# The four runtime aborts a test can reach, and the difference between an abort
# and a failed assertion in the report: an abort has no `actual`/`expected`
# pair, because there was no comparison — the runtime threw with a sentence.
#
# The `cli/tests/crash` corpus already holds these four as *programs*, where an
# abort ends the process. This case is the other half: inside `buri test` the
# JavaScript runner catches them, so an abort is one test's failure and the
# suite carries on. The last test asserts exactly that, and would be the first
# thing to notice if a future runner started stopping at the first abort — the
# way the native runner has to (`commands/test.rs::run_native`).
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "a runtime abort inside a test is reported with its own words and no diff, and the tests after it still run"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/arith", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "fail.txt"
  stream: OUT
}
